><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>When compared with non-disabled runners, runners with brainimpairment have slower sprint speeds and a range of gait param</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>eters that are characteristic of submaximal running speeds innon-disabled runners, including decreased stride and step length,decreased flight time, increased contact time.•Emerging evidence from studies of runners with brain impair-ment and non-disabled runners at matched running speedsindicates that runners with brain impairment may compensatefor a reduced step length by increasing their cadence, and thatreduced power generation at the ankle may lead to increased useof the hip musculature.•Limited research, small sample sizes and population heterogene-ity resulting from variability in type, severity and distribution ofmotor impairment is a particularly significant barrier to mean-ingful interpretation of biomechanical analyses of runners withbrain impairment.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="0" rect="637.098694,61.292694,821.025574,285.146729"
/></highlight
